</ul>
<div class="d-panels font-secondary-light">
<div id="aip_a" class="d-panel p" style="display: none">
<div class="p" id="A1">
<sup>1</sup>Quotient Therapeutics, Inc., Cambridge, MA, USA</div>
<div id="A2">
<sup>2</sup>Flagship Pioneering, Inc., Cambridge, MA, USA</div>
<div id="A3">
<sup>3</sup>Wellcome Sanger Institute, Wellcome Genome Campus, Hinxton, UK</div>
<div id="A4">
<sup>4</sup>Children’s Research Institute, Departments of Pediatrics and Internal Medicine, Simmons Comprehensive Cancer Center, Center for Regenerative Science and Medicine, University of Texas Southwestern Medical Center, Dallas, TX, USA</div>
<div id="A5">
<sup>5</sup>Work done while at Quotient Therapeutics</div>
<div class="author-notes p"><div class="fn" id="CR1">
<sup>*</sup><p class="display-inline">Correspondence: <span>hao.zhu@utsouthwestern.edu</span></p>
</div></div>
</div>
<div id="clp_a" class="d-panel p" style="display: none">
<p>This is an open access article under the CC BY license (<a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/" class="usa-link usa-link--external" data-ga-action="click_feat_suppl" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/</a>).</p>
<div class="p"><a href="/about/copyright/" class="usa-link">PMC Copyright notice</a></div>
</div>
</div>
<div>PMCID: PMC13694181  NIHMSID: NIHMS2150936  PMID: <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41795441/" class="usa-link">41795441</a>
</div>
<div class="ra xbox p" role="complementary" aria-label="Related or updated information about this article"><div>The publisher's version of this article is available at <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cell.2026.01.032" class="usa-link usa-link--external" data-ga-action="click_feat_suppl" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cell</a>
</div></div>
</div></section></section><section aria-label="Article content"><section class="body main-article-body"><section class="abstract" id="ABS1"><h2>SUMMARY</h2>
<p id="P1">Somatic mutations, or genetic changes occurring in cells after conception, are widespread in healthy tissues but are conventionally viewed as signs of pre-cancer or simply a consequence of aging. However, an emerging body of work has shown that somatic mutations can drive or protect against disease, which could inspire novel therapeutic strategies. The unexpected depth of genetic diversity within individuals also provides a massive substrate for discovering mutant genes selected for by disease. For instance, mutant hematopoietic cells can exacerbate inflammatory disease, and mutant hepatocytes can protect against liver disease. This suggests that somatic mutations, whether maladaptive or beneficial, could provide crucial insights into disease mechanisms, history, and reversal strategies. Somatic genetics offers a powerful, complementary approach to traditional germline genetics, which has had an enormous impact on biomedicine and drug development. This review explores the factors that shape the landscape of somatic mosaicism and discusses somatic mutations that cause or protect from disease. We highlight how somatic mutations are becoming a key discovery engine for disease genetics, moving rapidly toward drug target identification and clinical translation.</p></section><section id="S1"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">INTRODUCTION: SOMATIC MUTATIONS ARE WIDESPREAD IN HEALTH AND DISEASE</h2>
<p id="P2">Somatic mutations can occur at any stage of development, and those that occur early in embryogenesis can lead to striking examples that illustrate the potency of these mutations. In Proteus syndrome, a large fraction of cells in affected individuals contain activating mutations in <em>AKT1</em> or inactivating mutations in <em>PTEN</em>, leading to debilitating limb and organ overgrowths. <sup><a href="#R1" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R1">1</a>,<a href="#R2" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R2">2</a></sup> Arteriovenous malformations (AVMs), or large vascular tangles that can arise in any part of the body, are frequently caused by somatic mutations in phosphatidylinositol 3-kinase (<em>PI3K</em>) or <em>KRAS</em>. <sup><a href="#R3" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R3">3</a>,<a href="#R4" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R4">4</a></sup> Café au lait macules, associated with neurofibromatosis, are mosaic hyperpigmented skin lesions that are caused by loss of <em>NF1</em> heterozygosity. Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease is a germline syndrome whereby innumerable cysts form after the somatic loss of a second copy of <em>PKD1</em> or <em>PKD2</em>. <sup><a href="#R5" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R5">5</a></sup> While these syndromes are morphologically striking, perhaps the most devastating examples of somatic mosaicism occur in sporadic cancers. Somatic mutations are necessary for the development of most cancers, and a major effort in modern oncology seeks to design treatments based on the genes that are somatically mutated in cancers.</p>
<p id="P3">In healthy individuals, mutations accumulate steadily in all somatic cells throughout life. <sup><a href="#R6" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R6">6</a>,<a href="#R7" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R7">7</a></sup> Most of this spontaneous genetic variation is driven by endogenous mutational processes that arise from the standard biochemical processes of cellular function, such as spontaneous deamination of methylated cytosines, DNA replication or repair errors, and reactive oxygen species. These endogenous mutational processes begin from the moment of fertilization and continue throughout life, even in post-mitotic cells (<a href="#F1" class="usa-link">Figure 1</a>). <sup><a href="#R8" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R8">8</a></sup> As a result, the rate of mutation accumulation is generally constant over time such that there is a linear correlation between age and mutation burden in most tissues. On average, each adult has approximately 1,000–2,000 mutations per cell and accumulates 20–50 new mutations/cell/year (<a href="#F1" class="usa-link">Figure 1</a>). However, there is up to 10-fold variation in mutation burden across different somatic organs, resulting from tissue-to-tissue and cell-to-cell variation in the activity of endogenous mutational processes. <sup><a href="#R9" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R9">9</a>–<a href="#R11" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R11">11</a></sup> Tissues subject to additional exogenous mutagen exposures, such as skin (sun), <sup><a href="#R12" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R12">12</a></sup> liver (alcohol), <sup><a href="#R13" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R13">13</a>,<a href="#R14" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R14">14</a></sup> lung (pollution, tobacco), <sup><a href="#R15" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R15">15</a></sup> esophagus (alcohol and tobacco), <sup><a href="#R16" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R16">16</a></sup> and bladder (alcohol and tobacco), <sup><a href="#R17" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R17">17</a></sup> harbor increased mutation burden.</p>
<figure class="fig xbox font-sm" id="F1"><h3 class="obj_head">Figure 1. Somatic mutations are ubiquitous, permitting the sampling of most gene perturbations at the organ level.</h3>
<p class="img-box line-height-none margin-x-neg-2 tablet:margin-x-0 text-center"><img class="graphic" src="https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/64c7/13694181/635977c6e515/nihms-2150936-f0001.webp" loading="lazy" height="562" width="1599" alt="Figure 1."></p>
<div class="p text-right font-secondary"><a href="figure/F1/" class="usa-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open in a new tab</a></div>
<figcaption><p id="P53">The number of genes was assumed to be 20,000 (<a href="https://gencodegenes.org" class="usa-link usa-link--external" data-ga-action="click_feat_suppl" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">gencodegenes.org</a>), and the fraction of coding regions in the genome was 1% (<a href="https://genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Gene" class="usa-link usa-link--external" data-ga-action="click_feat_suppl" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Gene</a>). Mutation burden in skin was calculated based on a cohort of donors of Singaporean descent, who have lower UV-related mutation burdens. <sup><a href="#R18" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R18">18</a></sup></p></figcaption></figure><p id="P4">While most mutations do not change the cell’s phenotype and are functionally inconsequential, some will inevitably land in critical coding regions or regulatory elements of the genome and exert phenotypic effects. Such phenotypic variation is the substrate upon which Darwinian selection can act, resulting in the disappearance or expansion of clones with altered fitness. As a consequence, normal tissues from healthy individuals can show the emergence of clones that carry positively selected somatic mutations, so-called “driver” mutations. By the age of 70 years, about a third of sun-exposed skin cells, <sup><a href="#R12" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R12">12</a></sup> about 50% of esophageal epithelial cells, <sup><a href="#R16" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R16">16</a></sup> and about 60% of endometrial gland cells <sup><a href="#R19" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R19">19</a></sup> will carry at least one driver mutation. In many organs, these driver mutations overlap between normal and malignant tissues, suggesting that the earliest events in cancer evolution are already detectable in normal tissues. However, many driver mutations in normal tissues are not seen, or are seen with reduced frequency in cancers, <sup><a href="#R16" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R16">16</a>,<a href="#R20" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R20">20</a></sup> and therefore do not participate in transformation. Disease processes represent additional selective pressures on mutant clones. The landscape of somatic mutations has been studied for conditions such as chronic liver disease, <sup><a href="#R13" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R13">13</a>,<a href="#R14" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R14">14</a>,<a href="#R21" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R21">21</a></sup> inflammatory bowel disease (IBD), <sup><a href="#R20" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R20">20</a>,<a href="#R22" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R22">22</a>,<a href="#R23" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R23">23</a></sup> psoriasis, <sup><a href="#R24" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R24">24</a></sup> and a small number of monogenic germline disorders. <sup><a href="#R25" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R25">25</a>–<a href="#R27" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R27">27</a></sup> Several general concepts emerge from these early studies: (1) diseases often select for a different repertoire of driver mutations than seen in healthy tissues and cancers, (2) clones often grow to larger sizes in disease tissue than seen in healthy tissues, and (3) convergent selection of the same driver mutations within and across patients is frequent. We will devote much of this review toward a discussion of somatic mutations identified in disease and what they might mean for clinical translation.</p></section><section id="S2"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">INTRINSIC AND EXTRINSIC FACTORS THAT SHAPE THE LANDSCAPE OF SOMATIC MOSAICISM</h2>
<section id="S3"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Tissue architecture and proliferation regulate somatic mosaicism</h3>
<p id="P5">Just as geographic factors play a crucial role in speciation, <sup><a href="#R28" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R28">28</a></sup> the process by which new biological species arise, organ-specific architecture could also determine the degree of somatic diversity. This topic is in the early stages of exploration <sup><a href="#R29" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R29">29</a></sup> but could lead to predictions about the size of mutant clones, the number of genes under selection, and the overall genetic diversity within an organ. The hematopoietic system has few geographic and space limitations, and blood mutations that confer the greatest fitness benefits can expand without spatial limits. Similarly, the skin and esophagus contain sheets of squamous tissues in which somatic mutant clones can expand two-dimensionally without restricted borders. These less restrictive boundaries allow for larger clone sizes and variant allele frequencies (VAFs). <sup><a href="#R29" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R29">29</a>,<a href="#R30" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R30">30</a></sup> Liver lobules provide an interesting contrast since hepatocytes are constrained by lobular boundaries, which become fibrotic barriers in chronic disease. Similarly, the intestinal crypt-villus structure limits the degree of stem cell expansion across the tissue. <sup><a href="#R31" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R31">31</a></sup> These gastrointestinal tissues contain innumerable clones that would be predicted to allow for increased genetic diversity and divergent evolutionary outcomes. <sup><a href="#R30" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R30">30</a></sup> In contrast with clonal hematopoiesis, where the same genes are recurrently mutated in most patients, <sup><a href="#R32" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R32">32</a>,<a href="#R33" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R33">33</a></sup> cirrhotic livers show high genetic diversity, <sup><a href="#R13" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R13">13</a>,<a href="#R14" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R14">14</a>,<a href="#R21" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R21">21</a></sup> where sampling of different sites within a single liver can reveal disparate genes under selection. Similarly, squamous tissues like skin and esophagus, with fewer geographic constraints, demonstrate dominance of <em>NOTCH1</em> and <em>TP53</em> mutant clones. <sup><a href="#R12" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R12">12</a>,<a href="#R16" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R16">16</a></sup> Other tissue-intrinsic variables certainly contribute to somatic genetic diversity. The degree of cell turnover within a tissue can influence diversity, with blood and intestine possessing rapid turnover, while the brain is predominantly post-mitotic. As discussed, increased mutagenesis rates in tissues such as the liver could also contribute to clonal diversity. Strong extrinsic pressures that lead to positive or purifying selection can lead to reduced genetic diversity and are discussed below. Mutagenesis, clonal selection, and architectural constraints can operate together to shape somatic evolution. <sup><a href="#R34" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R34">34</a></sup> To isolate how architecture contributes to the somatic mutation landscape, it will be important to theoretically and experimentally model somatic evolution in tissues with unrestricted vs. restricted structures.</p></section><section id="S4"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Inflammation shapes and is shaped by somatic mosaicism</h3>
<p id="P6">Selective pressures are evolutionary forces that influence the survival of clones, and new selective pressures may arise during aging or disease pathogenesis. Inflammation is one of the selective pressures that increase clonal hematopoiesis of indeterminate potential (CHIP). CHIP, defined as the presence of a clonal population comprising more than 4% of the peripheral blood, is present in more than 15% of individuals in their 70s and is associated with increased risks of leukemia, <sup><a href="#R32" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R32">32</a>,<a href="#R33" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R33">33</a></sup> cardiovascular disease, and ischemic stroke. <sup><a href="#R35" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R35">35</a></sup> While CHIP is strongly associated with these and other inflammatory conditions, it has been very difficult to establish causality based on sequencing data alone. Some insights into causality have been gained through mouse models. While CHIP may drive atherosclerosis, there is also mathematical and experimental evidence indicating that atherosclerosis might cause clonal expansions in CHIP. <sup><a href="#R36" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R36">36</a></sup> In rodent models, inflammation has been shown to promote clonal expansion of <em>TET2</em> mutant cells through interleukin (IL)-6 and tumor necrosis factor α (TNF-α) activity. <sup><a href="#R37" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R37">37</a>,<a href="#R38" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R38">38</a></sup> Mycobacteria, which produce interferon gamma (INFg), can cause expansion of <em>DNMT3A</em>-deficient multipotent progenitors in murine models, thereby demonstrating that inflammation from an infectious source can promote the emergence of CHIP. <sup><a href="#R39" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R39">39</a></sup> In turn, Wang et al. showed that in mouse models, <em>DNMT3A</em> mutations in CHIP caused increased periodontal disease and arthritis through increased osteoclastogenesis and IL-17. <sup><a href="#R40" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R40">40</a></sup> In tandem with human CHIP studies, experimental models suggest that inflammation and CHIP are ensnared in a complex feedback loop, with inflammation driving CHIP and CHIP promoting inflammation.</p>
<p id="P7">Inflammation may also impact the landscape of somatic mutations in solid organs. Intestinal epithelial cells in ulcerative colitis patients have more somatic mutations compared with healthy controls. <sup><a href="#R20" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R20">20</a>,<a href="#R22" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R22">22</a>,<a href="#R23" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R23">23</a></sup> Even within an individual ulcerative colitis patient, cells in the descending colon, which is more inflammatory, harbor more somatic mutations than cells in the ascending colon. <sup><a href="#R20" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R20">20</a>,<a href="#R22" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R22">22</a></sup> Similarly, chronic inflammation is also associated with increased somatic mutations in gastric epithelial cells. <sup><a href="#R41" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R41">41</a></sup> Conversely, in Barrett’s esophagus patients, fewer somatic mutations were found in those who continually used non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs. <sup><a href="#R42" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R42">42</a></sup> Despite experimental evidence, the degree to which somatic mosaicism (i.e., CHIP) is the cause of inflammatory diseases and vice versa is uncertain, illustrating the challenge of understanding somatic mutations and their relationships with disease.</p></section><section id="S5"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Chemicals, toxins, and chemotherapies are selective pressures that mediate clonal expansion</h3>
<p id="P8">Some chemicals act as carcinogens by increasing mutagenesis, but carcinogens can also select clones through “promoter” mechanisms. Many carcinogens, originally thought of as mutagens, likely promote cancer through altering the selective landscape experienced by tissues, thereby facilitating clonal expansion. <sup><a href="#R43" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R43">43</a></sup> In lung cancer, pollution could be promoting cancer initiation by increasing inflammation through IL-1β, which leads to the expansion of pre-existing mutant <em>KRAS</em> clones. <sup><a href="#R44" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R44">44</a></sup> The larger the clone, the greater the likelihood of a second hit mutation that drives malignant transformation. Similarly, smoking and alcohol are associated with increased mosaicism in the non-malignant lung, <sup><a href="#R15" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R15">15</a></sup> liver, <sup><a href="#R14" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R14">14</a></sup> esophagus, <sup><a href="#R16" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R16">16</a></sup> and bladder, <sup><a href="#R17" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R17">17</a></sup> likely in part by altering selective pressures (<a href="#F2" class="usa-link">Figure 2</a>). Another example is therapy-related clonal hematopoiesis, which is CHIP that is observed in patients treated with chemotherapy or radiation. In addition to creating new mutant clones, chemotherapy likely causes the expansion of pre-existing mutant clones. <sup><a href="#R45" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R45">45</a>–<a href="#R48" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R48">48</a></sup> Altogether, many exogenous agents shape the somatic mutation landscape not through mutagenesis, but instead through increasing cell turnover, creating niche space for regenerative clones, or by exerting new selective pressures. From an adaptive viewpoint, mutations might point to mechanisms of resistance or enhanced regeneration after exposure to promoter agents. An extension of this concept is that long-term exposures to medications could lead to altered selection of somatic clones that may provide useful clinical information.</p>
<figure class="fig xbox font-sm" id="F2"><h4 class="obj_head">Figure 2. The landscape of somatic mutations is shaped by selective pressures unique to different diseases.</h4>
<p class="img-box line-height-none margin-x-neg-2 tablet:margin-x-0 text-center"><img class="graphic" src="https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/64c7/13694181/8053bc01483f/nihms-2150936-f0002.webp" loading="lazy" height="1128" width="1600" alt="Figure 2."></p>
<div class="p text-right font-secondary"><a href="figure/F2/" class="usa-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open in a new tab</a></div>
<figcaption><p id="P54">(A) The healthy liver harbors a patchwork of small clones, depicted as circles, each with its own somatic mutations. In patients with hepatic lipotoxicity from excess alcohol or carbohydrate consumption, mutations that impair <em>de novo</em> lipogenesis will protect hepatocytes. These will preferentially survive and expand to repopulate the damaged liver. In a patient with alpha-1-antitrypsin deficiency, somatic mutations in the <em>SERPINA1</em> gene can alleviate liver damage by preventing misfolded SERPINA1 protein from aggregating, thus improving liver cell survival.</p>
<p id="P55">(B) In the esophagus, even healthy individuals harbor somatic mutations, most prominently in <em>NOTCH1</em>/<em>2</em>, <em>PIK3CA</em>, and <em>TP53</em>. Clones with these mutations can expand in the context of metabolic syndrome, tobacco use, or excessive alcohol.</p></figcaption></figure></section><section id="S6"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Metabolic disease shapes somatic mosaicism in the liver and esophagus</h3>
<p id="P9">In modern society, a widespread exposure that underlies an extraordinary burden of disease is overnutrition, and it stands to reason that this would also shape somatic mosaicism. Chronic liver disease is an exemplar of how different environmental pressures could select for distinct somatic mutations. The most common etiologies are viral hepatitis (hepatitis B and hepatitis C), chronic alcohol consumption, and metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) (<a href="#F2" class="usa-link">Figure 2A</a>). These processes induce cycles of cell death and regeneration that over decades lead to liver cirrhosis, a clinical state where liver nodules are surrounded by bands of fibrosis. The combination of chronic toxicity and regeneration provides a trove of somatic mutations that influence clonal dynamics. Two groups reported somatically acquired driver mutations in regenerative nodules in chronic liver disease. <sup><a href="#R13" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R13">13</a>,<a href="#R14" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R14">14</a></sup> In a cohort of predominantly hepatitis C-associated cirrhosis patients, driver mutations in <em>PKD1</em>, <em>ARID1A</em>, and <em>KMT2D</em> were identified. In a cohort of alcohol-related or MASLD patients, driver mutations in <em>FOXO1</em>, <em>GPAM</em>, and <em>CIDEB</em> were found. Strikingly, the genes in MASLD are not classic cancer drivers, and the mutations are predicted to modulate the storage, synthesis, or oxidation of lipids in affected hepatocytes.</p>
<p id="P10">The MASLD studies demonstrated convergent evolution within patients but considerable divergence between patients, which could suggest that selective pressures differ between patients. A given patient with MASLD will typically show convergent evolution of multiple independent clones that carry mutations in the same driver gene. This convergence can lead to hundreds of grams of liver tissue affected by mutations in the same driver gene, indicating the potential to have organ-wide functional consequences. In support of this, murine studies show that loss of <em>Pkd1</em> in just 5% of the liver can lead to measurable metabolic benefits at the organismal level. <sup><a href="#R49" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R49">49</a></sup> Intriguingly, patients often differ in which genes are mutated—one might have many <em>FOXO1</em>-mutant clones, another may harbor <em>GPAM-</em>mutant clones, and a third could show a predominance of <em>CIDEB</em> mutations. The disease process in a given patient introduces a distinctive, organ-wide selective pressure that a limited repertoire of mutations can alleviate—this causes the within-patient convergence of drivers. In another patient, the disease process might introduce a different selective pressure, for which a different set of driver mutations emerges—this causes the between-patient divergence in mutations (<a href="#F2" class="usa-link">Figure 2A</a>). For example, one patient might arrive at MASLD through excess alcohol and carbohydrates, in which case hepatocytes with mutations targeting <em>de novo</em> lipogenesis genes (such as <em>GPAM</em>
<sup><a href="#R50" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R50">50</a></sup> ) would be more likely to survive and regenerate. Another patient might arrive at MASLD through dietary excess of fats, in which case <em>de novo</em> lipogenesis would be less important, but genes involved in lipid storage and oxidation, such as <em>CIDEB</em>, <sup><a href="#R51" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R51">51</a>,<a href="#R52" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R52">52</a></sup> might be more beneficial. Thus, the set of driver genes illustrates the genes and pathways that provide protection when mutated. Furthermore, the patterns of driver mutations observed in different patients provide clues to the subtypes of disease processes in the population and may be useful as companion diagnostics for targeted therapeutics. Experimental evidence for this concept was demonstrated for <em>CIDEB</em>, where loss-of-function mutations were more positively selected under some but not all diets that promote fatty liver disease. <sup><a href="#R51" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R51">51</a></sup></p>
<p id="P11">Other tissues also harbor undesirable mutations whose selection is sensitive to metabolic stresses. In the esophagus, there is a high frequency of <em>PIK3CA</em> mutations (<a href="#F2" class="usa-link">Figure 2B</a>). Herms et al. modeled <em>Pik3ca</em>
<sup><em>H1047R</em></sup> clones using knockin heterozygous mice and demonstrated that squamous clones containing these mutations expand with age by outcompeting wild-type (WT) clones. <sup><a href="#R53" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="R53">53</a></sup> Inducing diabetes contributed to the growth of <em>Pik3ca</em> mutant clones because insulin-resistant WT cells are less able to respond to circulating insulin, whereas mutant cells are less dependent on circulating insulin for pathway activation. Conversely, metformin treatment reduced the size of these clones. This elegantly demonstrated that environmental conditions such as obesity and diabetes can lead to the positive selection of mutant vs. WT clones. This could be one mechanism by which obesity promotes pre-cancer growth in some tissues.</p></section></section><section id="S7"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">SOMATIC MUTATIONS CAN DRIVE DISEASE</h2>
<p id="P12">Somatic mutations are emerging as unexpected drivers of diseases with previously idiopathic etiologies, i.e., focal epilepsies and autoimmunity. Disease-driving genes with clear pathogenic mechanisms represent actionable therapeutic targets (<a href="#F3" class="usa-link">Figure 3</a>; <a href="#T1" class="usa-link">Table 1</a>). These salient examples should stimulate the search for other somatic mutations that cause non-malignant diseases.</p>
<figure class="fig xbox font-sm" id="F3"><h3 class="obj_head">Figure 3. Somatic genomics uncovers the outcomes of evolutionary competitions within our tissues, which can drive disease, counter monogenic disease, or protect from common diseases.</h3>
<p class="img-box line-height-none margin-x-neg-2 tablet:margin-x-0 text-center"><img class="graphic" src="https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/64c7/13694181/e6a331795302/nihms-2150936-f0003.webp" loading="lazy" height="1073" width="1600" alt="Figure 3."></p>
